{"title":"Gateway Parking","sub":"A car park that's full by 8:30 and empty by 7pm","type":"Pricing","industry":"Real estate","geo":"Canada","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"Time-based pricing · capacity · segmentation","prompt":"Gateway runs an 800-space car park in downtown Vancouver at a flat $20 a day. On weekdays it's full by 8:30am and turns away about 200 cars; after 6pm it's mostly empty. The owner asks how to earn more from the same spaces.","clarify":[{"q":"Who parks?","a":"Mostly commuters who stay all day. Shoppers and visitors arrive after 10am and stay 2–4 hours, but find it full."},{"q":"Evenings?","a":"About 150 cars come in the evening for restaurants and events and pay the $20 day rate; that price puts others off."},{"q":"Research?","a":"Commuters would pay up to about $24 to guarantee a space. About 300 late-morning visitors a day would come at $16 for a stay of up to 4 hours. An $8 evening rate would bring about 400 evening cars."},{"q":"Turnover?","a":"Short-stay visitors free their space mid-afternoon, so two short stays can share a space."},{"q":"Days?","a":"Assume about 250 weekdays a year; ignore weekends."}],"structure":{"root":"How should Gateway price its spaces?","branches":[{"head":"Who uses the space when","items":["Commuters","Short-stay visitors","Evening"]},{"head":"Willingness to pay","items":["By group"]},{"head":"Capacity","items":["Spaces × time"]}]},"hypothesis":"A flat price ignores that the space is scarce in the morning and idle at night. Charging by time of day should raise revenue and serve more people.","questions":[{"q":"What does Gateway earn today?","exhibit":null,"answer":"<p>(800 daytime + 150 evening cars) × $20 × 250 weekdays = <b>$4.75M a year</b>.</p>"},{"q":"Design a time-based price list and estimate revenue.","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"388.46pt\" height=\"159.66pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 388.46 159.66\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.or